{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310647,"nonce":"ZaGA/9wxEJ3E6Av/Mm5+MJ+Na0s=","title":"WHY macros fail, measured — 72 macro-using corpus cases (11 PASS / 39 COMPILE_FAIL / 8 WRONG_ANSWER / 4 CRASH / 5 SKIP) split into FOUR ...","message":"Measured 2026-09-06 at 0483cba6. Basic macro machinery WORKS: `macro 1+1`,\n`$e`, `${e}`, `macro : Int`, `$v{n}`, a macro called from the same class, from\nanother class, declared after its call site, from an instance method, from a\nsubclass, and through the harness's packaged/class-path shape — all fine.\n\n**STATUS 2026-09-06: causes 2, 3 and 4 FIXED (4bc7c395, ecad216c, 0236df99).\nCause 1 (the parser) is NOT done — see the ATTEMPT note under it.**\n\n**1. Two reification forms the parser cannot read (11 cases).**\n`macro : $t` (a type ESCAPE in type position) -\u003e \"expected type, found '$t'\";\n`macro class Foo {...}` (type-declaration reification) -\u003e \"expected expression,\nfound 'class'\". The RD parser fails, falls back to the legacy parser, and THAT\nfails too, so the file yields \"No main function found\" — which is why these\nscore as `main present but not found: expected expression, found 'class'`.\n\nATTEMPT REVERTED: adding `$ident` to type position in BOTH parsers (there are\ntwo — `parser/src/rd/types.rs` is primary, `parser/src/haxe_parser_types.rs` is\nthe legacy fallback; the RD one is what the warning names) makes `macro : $t`\nPARSE, and the program then fails SILENTLY with no output, because reification\nnever substitutes it: `compiler/src/macro_system/reification.rs` does not touch\ntypes at all. A clean parse error became a silent failure, so it was reverted.\nDoing this properly needs three layers — parse, substitute in reification, and\nsplice back — and `macro class {...}` additionally needs an AST value for a type\ndeclaration. Feature-sized, not a patch.\n\n**2. A failing macro body is silently DISCARDED (12 cases).** The declaration\nvanishes and the ONLY diagnostic is `Cannot find name '\u003cmacro\u003e'` AT THE CALL\nSITE, so the cluster reads as name resolution and is not. Bisected: a body using\n`Type.getClass(...)` or `Type.getClassName(...)` kills it; `e.pos` and local vars\nare fine. So the `Type` reflection API is missing from the macro interpreter, and\nthe reporting hides which construct failed. FIX THE DIAGNOSTIC FIRST — it is\nmis-attributing every case in this bucket.\n\n**3. Missing Context methods (5 cases).** `toComplexType`, `storeTypedExpr` (x2),\n`getExpectedType`. The implemented set (`macro_system/context_api.rs`) is 24:\nalias currentPos defineType defined definedValue enum error fatalError\ngetBuildFields getDefines getLocalClass getLocalMethod getLocalModule getPosInfos\ngetType info interface makeExpr makeMonomorph makePosition parse reportError\ntypeExpr typeof unify warning.\n\n**5. `eval` IS DEFINED ON PURPOSE, and the preprocessor is NOT the problem.**\n`compilation/mod.rs` `preprocessor_config()` inserts `eval` (masquerading as\nHaxe's macro-interpreter target so the stdlib picks native branches) and\n`rayzor`; `RAYZOR_NO_EVAL_DEFINE=1` turns it off and IMMEDIATELY breaks Math\n(`Cannot find name 'Number'` from the JS bootstrap) and cascades into FPHelper,\nexactly as the comment there predicts. `macro` is NOT defined, and `#if macro`\nblocks drop correctly -- verified by putting an undefined name inside one.\n`haxe.macro.MacroStringTools` fails for its OWN reason: `toFieldExpr` (line 70)\nuses `macro $i{s}` / `macro $e.$s` inside an ORDINARY function, i.e. reification\nas a RUNTIME value, which is not lowered. Six-line repro:\n`static function f(sl) return Lambda.fold(sl, function(s,e) return e == null ? (macro $i{s}) : (macro $e.$s), null);`\nSTILL OPEN: the `error[IMPORT]` summary prints positions ~18 lines off even\nafter 583beb8a fixed the preprocessor; the direct (non-import) diagnostic path\nis correct, so the import summary computes its own position.\n\n**4. `.match(pattern)` is lowered as an ordinary CALL (4+ cases).** The pattern's\n`_` resolves as a variable: `Cannot find name '_'`. This is NOT macro-specific —\n14 corpus files use `.match(`. It is why `haxe.macro.MacroStringTools` fails to\ncompile (`e.expr.match(EConst(CString(_, SingleQuotes)))` at lines 51-52), and\nevery case importing it inherits `error[IMPORT] ... failed to compile`.\n\nSee [[feature_deferred_typed_macros.md]], [[feature_macro_custom_annotations.md]].\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): WHY macros fail, MEASURED: 4 causes. (1) parser can't read `macro : $t` / `macro class{}`; (2) a failing macro body is DISCARDED and reported as `Cannot find name` AT THE CALL SITE (Type.getClass/getClassName trigger it) — the cluster is NOT name resolution; (3) 4 missing Context methods; (4) `.match(pattern)` lowered as a plain call ⇒ `_` unresolved, breaks MacroStringTools. Basic macro machinery WORKS.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_macro_failure_map_2026_09_06` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}